From 470eff5a1ed5f9157f05741ca672ec36c4be4b98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 12:51:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assurance.collect: the on-ramp, and the noise problem that
 kills drift tools
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WHY
  Everything built so far assumes a manifest exists, and until now that meant
  hand-writing JSON — so nobody would have used any of it. But a collector is
  only worth having if it solves the problem that quietly ruins every
  configuration-drift tool in this industry: vendor exports are not
  byte-stable. They stamp themselves with the export time, the operator, a
  sequence number, a checksum of themselves. Hash the file as it is and every
  weekly collection reports drift on every machine, the customer stops reading
  the report inside a fortnight, and the week the firmware really did change is
  the week nobody looks.

WHAT
  src/assurance/collect/normalise.py  four declared rules — raw, text_excluding,
                                       json_excluding, zip_members — each
                                       reporting exactly what it dropped
  src/assurance/collect/plan.py       what to look for, where, and how to hash
                                       it: written once per machine type
  src/assurance/collect/collector.py  run the plan, produce the manifest
  src/assurance/collect/volatility.py collect twice, find the rules that report
                                       the export's own noise, and suggest the
                                       patterns that fix them
  src/assurance/collect/cli.py        check-plan / probe / run
  examples/collect/plan-ar7.json      a probed, stable plan
  deploy/assurance/COLLECT.md         the method, and what it refuses to do

CUSTOMER VALUE
  An engineer drops the exports in a folder and runs one command; a sealed
  manifest exists for that serial number, at tier validated, ready for
  machinery and fleet with no further work. One plan covers forty units.

  And the probe: three of four items in the shipped example would have produced
  false drift every single week. It found all three, showed the lines that
  moved, and suggested the exclusions. No drift tool ships this step and every
  one of them needs it.

RISK
  A rule that drops bytes must say why, or it is refused — an unexplained
  exclusion cannot be told apart from hiding a change. A declared pattern that
  matched nothing is a warning, not a success. The rule's fingerprint is part of
  the item's identity, so a digest taken under a changed rule is visibly a
  different measurement. A required item that is not found is LEFT OUT of the
  manifest and reported loudly, never invented. There is no scanner mode: a tool
  that guesses what is safety-relevant guesses wrong in both directions.

ROLLBACK
  git revert this commit. Outside assurance/collect it touches one CLI domain
  and one ruff ignore. Nothing else in the package changes.

TESTS
  504 passed (51 new), ruff clean over src/assurance and the new tests.
  The end-to-end case is asserted both ways: two exports of an unchanged
  machine produce an identical configuration hash, and a 1500 -> 1200 mm zone
  change produces exactly one finding carrying SF-01 — while three other items'
  export headers moved in the same collection and none is reported.

SUCCESS CONDITION
  One integrator runs the probe against their own exports, sees which of their
  files are stamped, and realises nobody has ever been able to tell them that.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
